;; What "the release that was admitted is the release that gets applied" means,
;; as checkable data.
;;
;; aiueos.update takes :publisher-admitted? as a bare boolean and checks it once.
;; Nothing in that seam binds the boolean to a release, so a verdict about one
;; release can advance an update carrying another's bytes. The unit here is an
;; ADMISSION -- a publisher verdict stamped with the release identity and the
;; time it was taken -- and every step re-checks the stamp.
[{:db/id -1
  :aiueos.ota/id :aiueos/default-ota
  :aiueos.ota/source-files ["src/grant/ota.cljk"]
  :aiueos.ota/composes ["src/grant/publisher.cljk" "src/grant/update.cljk"]
  :aiueos.ota/admission-fields [:manifest-id :sequence :taken-ms :verdict]
  :aiueos.ota/rechecked-every-step? true
  :aiueos.ota/admission-expires? true
  :aiueos.ota/decision-shapes #{:grant :deny}
  :aiueos.ota/deny-reasons #{:no-admission :admission-for-another-release
                             :admission-expired :manifest-id-missing
                             :artifact-digest-mismatch :artifact-missing
                             :release-not-admitted}
  :aiueos.ota/reasons-pass-through
  "publisher and update reasons are returned unchanged; a caller should not have
   to guess which layer refused, and re-labelling would lose that"
  :aiueos.ota/missing-artifact-is-a-mismatch? true
  :aiueos.ota/outcomes #{:committed :rolled-back :refused}
  :aiueos.ota/receipt-faults #{:manifest-id-mismatch :admission-mismatch
                               :unknown-outcome :recovery-moved
                               :swap-did-not-happen :artifacts-not-covered}
  :aiueos.ota/receipt-reports-all-faults? true
  :aiueos.ota/swap-check-scoped-to :committed
  :aiueos.ota/assurance-gaps
  ["Identity is supplied, not computed. :manifest-id is a digest the caller
    made; this namespace guarantees the same id flows through every step, not
    that the id is the right one. A provider that computes it wrongly makes
    every check here agree about the wrong thing."
   "Nothing here fetches bytes, hashes them, writes a partition or reboots.
    The A/B write and the OTA fetch path are still provider mechanism and are
    still unimplemented -- this closes the decision seam between them, not the
    seam to the disk."
   "Artifact digests are compared as opaque values. Whether :sha256 really is a
    SHA-256 of the fetched bytes is the provider's claim."]}]
